# Which units a Hangar environment starts.
#
# mkosi runs `systemctl preset-all` after the postinstall script, so presets,
# not `systemctl enable`, decide what is enabled in the finished image. The
# distributions disagree on the default for a unit no preset mentions --
# Debian's policy enables it, RHEL's disables it -- so every unit Hangar cares
# about is named here rather than left to that default.
#
# Preset files are read in lexical order and the first matching rule wins, so
# the 00- prefix puts these ahead of the distribution's own policy. A unit that
# is not installed is skipped, which is what lets one file serve every base.

enable docker.service
enable containerd.service
enable hangar-agent.service
enable hangar-smoke.service
enable hangar-desktop.service

# Debian-family bases use networkd, RHEL-family bases NetworkManager.
enable systemd-networkd.service
enable systemd-resolved.service
enable NetworkManager.service

# Nothing in an environment needs to block on the network being up, and these
# add several seconds to every boot.
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service
disable NetworkManager-wait-online.service
